1+\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $1-\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $1-\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}} & =\\frac{3\\text{i}}{1+\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1-\\text{i}}{1-\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}-3(-1)}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\text{i}+3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534824 true] [17592186060371 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534824 true] [17592186060371 1100 "If $z_{1} = 2 - 5\\textrm{i}$ and $z_{2} = 6 + 3\\textrm{i}$, then $z_{1}$ divided by $z_{2}$ equals" 13194139534824 false] [17592186060371 1100 "If ${z}_{1}=2-5\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=6+3\\text{i}$, then ${z}_{1}$ divided by ${z}_{2}$ equals" 13194139534824 true] [17592186060371 1145 "First, we set up the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is $6+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $6-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $6-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{z}_{1}}{{z}_{2}} & =\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-5\\text{i}}{6+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{6-3\\text{i}}{6-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-6\\text{i}-30\\text{i}+15{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{6}^{2}-{3}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-36\\text{i}+15(-1)}{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-36\\text{i}-15}{36+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-36\\text{i}}{45}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{align